.TH HEAD 1 2026-10-31 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
head \- print the first part of files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B head
.RB [ \-qv ]
.RB [ \-n
.RI [\-] count ]
.RB [ \-c
.RI [\-] count ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B head
.BI \- count
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B head
writes the first part of each
.I file
to standard output: by default the first 10 lines.
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
This
.B head
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
A
.I file
that cannot be opened or read is reported on standard error, and the
remaining files are still written.
.SS Counts
A count is a decimal number, optionally followed by one of the suffixes
.BR b ,
.BR k ,
or
.BR m ,
which multiply it by 512, 1024, or 1048576.
Suffixes are lowercase.
The result must be from 0 to 18446744073709551615.
Hexadecimal, a leading
.BR + ,
and white space are not accepted.
A count that is malformed or out of range is an error, reported before
any file is read.
.PP
With
.BR \-n ,
.B head
counts lines, which end at an LF.
A CR is an ordinary byte.
A last line without an LF is written as it is, without adding one.
With
.BR \-c ,
it counts bytes.
A count of 0 writes nothing.
.PP
A count that begins with
.B \-
counts from the end: everything except the last
.I count
lines or bytes is written.
.B head
must read all of its input to find the end, and keeps the last
.I count
plus 1 lines or bytes in memory.
When the input has no more than
.I count
lines or bytes, nothing is written.
In this mode with
.BR \-n ,
a NUL byte also ends a line, and is not written.
.PP
If counts are given more than once, the last one is used.
If any of them is given with
.BR \-c ,
it counts bytes, and if any begins with
.BR \- ,
it counts from the end.
.SS Headers
With more than one
.I file
operand, or with
.BR \-v ,
.B head
writes a header before the output of each file: a line of the form
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
==> name <==
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I name
is the operand as given, or
.B standard input
for standard input.
Each header after the one for the first operand starts with an LF.
That gives an empty line between files, or ends the last line of a file
that had no final LF.
This holds even when the first operand could not be opened.
.B \-q
turns headers off.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-qn5 ,
and an option's argument may be attached or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
The first operand also ends them: a later argument such as
.B \-n
is a file name.
A lone
.B \-
is an operand.
.TP
.BI "\-n " count
Write the first
.I count
lines.
The default is 10.
With a leading
.BR \- ,
write all but the last
.I count
lines.
See
.BR Counts .
.TP
.BI "\-c " count
Write the first
.I count
bytes instead of lines.
With a leading
.BR \- ,
write all but the last
.I count
bytes.
.TP
.B \-q
Never write headers.
.TP
.B \-v
Always write headers, even for one operand or for standard input with no
operands.
The last of
.B \-q
and
.B \-v
on the command line wins.
.TP
.BI \- count
As the first argument only, the same as
.BI "\-n " count
except that
.I count
cannot begin with
.BR \- .
Other options may follow, as in
.BR "head \-5 \-q a.txt b.txt" ,
but the digits cannot be grouped with them:
.B \-5q
is an error.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or count, a
.I file
could not be opened or read, memory ran out, or standard output could not
be written.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed while
.B head
was writing to it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write the first 3 lines of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ head \-n 3 notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write the first line of each of two files, with headers:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ head \-n 1 a.txt b.txt
==> a.txt <==
first line of a
\&
==> b.txt <==
first line of b
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write the first line of every text file, without headers:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ head \-qn1 *.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write all but the last line of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ head \-n \-1 data.csv
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy the first 64 KiB of a file to another file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ head \-c 64k big.bin >first.bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the first 16 bytes of a program in hex:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ head \-c 16 program.exe | xxd
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep the five smallest numbers of a list:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sort \-n sizes.txt | head \-5
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR head :
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options other than
.BR \-\-help ,
no
.BR \-\-version ,
and no
.BR \-z .
.IP \(bu 2
Options are not accepted after the first operand.
.IP \(bu 2
Counts are decimal with the lowercase suffixes
.BR b ,
.BR k ,
and
.B m
only.
GNU suffixes such as
.BR K ,
.BR kB ,
and
.BR MB ,
and a leading
.BR + ,
are errors.
.PP
With a leading
.BR \- ,
a line count of 268435455 or more (536870911 or more in the 32-bit
kit), or a byte count of 2147483647 or more, is refused with
.B count is too big
before any file is read.
.PP
When
.B head
stops before the end of a seekable standard input, it leaves it just
after the last byte it used, so a command that shares the redirected
standard input with
.B head
continues from there.
.PP
Files and standard input and output are binary, so CR LF line endings
and Ctrl-Z characters are copied unchanged.
.PP
File names may use drive letters, backslashes, and forward slashes, and
a header shows the name as given.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
The device names
.IR /dev/null ,
.IR /dev/zero ,
.IR /dev/urandom ,
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.I /dev/tty
(the console), and
.I /dev/fd/N
work as on Unix.
A directory operand fails with
.BR "Is a directory" .
.PP
When standard output is a console, text is converted for display from
the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later,
to the console's code page, unless
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
or
.B LANG
is
.BR C .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR less (1),
.BR sed (1),
.BR sort (1),
.BR tail (1),
.BR xxd (1)
